[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Blue-Green Deployments and Zero-Downtime Releases in CI/CD"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Blue-Green deployment is a release management strategy that reduces downtime and risk by running two identical production environments simultaneously, designated as Blue and Green. At any given time, only one environment serves live production traffic while the other remains idle or hosts the staging deployment."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Blue Environment: The current active version of your application serving real users."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Green Environment: The new version of your application deployed and tested in isolation."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Router/Load Balancer: The traffic controller that switches user requests between the Blue and Green environments instantaneously."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Step-by-Step Implementation"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To achieve zero-downtime releases, your CI/CD pipeline must automate the deployment to the inactive environment, run health checks, and update the load balancer routing configuration."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"docker build -t myapp:v2 .
docker run -d --name green-env -p 8081:8080 myapp:v2"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the Green environment is running on port 8081, execute automated integration and smoke tests against it to ensure stability."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"curl -f http://localhost:8081/health || exit 1"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"After successful validation, update your reverse proxy or load balancer configuration to point incoming traffic to the Green environment."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"upstream backend {
    server green-env:8080;
}
# Reload Nginx to shift traffic
nginx -s reload"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Rollback Strategy"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"If critical errors occur in the Green environment post-release, rolling back is as simple as reverting the load balancer configuration back to the Blue environment."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"upstream backend {
    server blue-env:8080;
}
nginx -s reload"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Blue-Green deployments eliminate deployment windows and drastically reduce the blast radius of failed releases. By integrating this pattern into your CI/CD workflow, you ensure continuous availability and a seamless user experience."}]
